# November 20, 2026: a first scatter of places across Alberta outside the Crowsnest Pass, from the Peace River country
# (Dunvegan) and the North Saskatchewan (St. Albert, Victoria Settlement, Rocky Mountain House, Lac La Biche) to the
# badlands (East Coulee, Rosedale, Big Valley), the irrigated plains (Brooks, Medicine Hat), Lethbridge, Calgary, Edmonton,
# the foothills (Bar U, Nordegg) and Markerville. Region ab taken with tools/choose.py --claim --thinnest. Sources opened in
# this session: the Canadian Register of Historic Places statement for each designated place (historicplaces.ca, read with
# tools/page.py --all; the register ids come from the {{HPC row}} templates of Wikipedia's Alberta historic-place lists);
# Wikipedia as raw text (tools/wiki_raw.py); and the page of whoever runs each place for hours (the Government of Alberta
# historic-site pages, the Arts and Heritage Foundation of St. Albert, Lac La Biche Mission, the Atlas Coal Mine, Medalta,
# Clearwater County for Nordegg, the Canadian Northern Society, Lougheed House, the City of Lethbridge, and Parks Canada's
# 2025 Rocky Mountain House activity guide). Parks Canada's own web pages would not open from here.
from data_b import N
from data_ma import W
def L(t,u): return [t,u]
def HP(t,i): return ['Canadian Register of Historic Places: '+t,'https://www.historicplaces.ca/en/rep-reg/place-lieu.aspx?id=%d'%i]
BATCH={'key':'ab_klbx','label':'Across Alberta: missions, fur posts, mines and mansions','added':'2026-11-20 12:19'}
PLACES=[
# ---- Edmonton and the North Saskatchewan ----
N('father-lacombe-chapel','Father Lacombe Chapel','St. Albert','ab','worship','standing',53.63859,-113.63091,'site','open','drive','','1861',
  'In 1861 Father Albert Lacombe and Bishop Alexandre Taché chose a site on the north side of the Sturgeon River for a new mission, which Taché named St. Albert after Lacombe\'s patron saint. Its first building was this small log church, built that year; the Register calls it probably the oldest building still standing in Alberta. St. Albert grew into one of the province\'s earliest farming settlements and in 1871 became the seat of a diocese covering a vast territory. The chapel became a Provincial Historic Resource in 1983.',
  'A small church of axe-hewn, squared and whitewashed timbers in the post-on-sill (Hudson\'s Bay Company) style on a fieldstone foundation, with a cedar-shingled gable roof crowned by a white cross and rough-sawn shiplap on the front. Inside: the original pit-sawn painted floorboards, exposed rafters and tie beams, whitewashed log walls and a simple panelled altar. Costumed interpreters also lead walks over Mission Hill to the historic bells, the grotto and the cemetery. The Bishop\'s Palace stands beside it.',
  'Alberta\'s oldest standing building, a log church from 1861.',
  'A Government of Alberta provincial historic site at 2 St. Vital Avenue, open seasonally from Victoria Day to Labour Day; admission free, donations welcome. Parking next to the chapel. Hours from the Arts and Heritage Foundation of St. Albert\'s locations page; outside the season and for groups, contact the Musée Héritage Museum.',
  [HP('Father Lacombe Church',5004),L('Arts and Heritage Foundation St. Albert: Father Lacombe Chapel','https://www.artsandheritage.ca/pages/father-lacombe-chapel')],
  brick='no',
  hours='Victoria Day to Labour Day: Wednesday to Sunday 10 a.m. to 5 p.m.'),
N('rutherford-house','Rutherford House','Edmonton','ab','landmark','standing',53.52755,-113.5203,'exact','open','drive','','1911',
  'Home of Alexander Cameron Rutherford, Alberta\'s first premier (1905 to 1910) and a founder of the University of Alberta, from its completion in 1911 until his death in 1941. It was built for $25,000 on a large lot in what was the City of Strathcona, and the family called it Achnacarry. In 1966 the university planned to demolish it for expansion; a public campaign, with the University Women\'s Club prominent in it, saved the house, and it is now a provincial historic site.',
  'A two-storey red brick house with sandstone trim and sills, parapetted gables, corbelled chimneys, two-storey bay windows each side of the door and a porch and balcony on paired Doric columns. Inside: oak woodwork and panelling, the original room and staircase layout, a stained glass skylight, and the library that held Rutherford\'s rare Canadiana. Shown by interpreters; the second floor is not wheelchair accessible.',
  'The first premier\'s brick mansion, saved from the university\'s bulldozers.',
  'A Government of Alberta provincial historic site at 11153 Saskatchewan Drive, on the north edge of the University of Alberta campus. Admission charged (adults $7 on its page). Call ahead to be sure an interpreter is free for a tour. Limited parking in front of the house; otherwise pay at the meters or the campus lots. No pets.',
  [W('Rutherford House'),HP('Rutherford House',5232),L('Rutherford House: visit','https://rutherfordhouse.ca/visit')],
  brick='yes',hours='Wednesday to Sunday 10 a.m. to 5 p.m.; closed December 24 to 26, January 1 and Easter Sunday'),
N('victoria-settlement-pakan','Victoria Settlement (Fort Victoria)','Smoky Lake','ab','landmark','standing',54.00384,-112.3981,'area','open','drive','','1862–1883',
  'The Methodist missionary George McDougall set up a mission here on the North Saskatchewan in 1862 and 1863, at a Cree meeting place on a well-used hunting ground. The Hudson\'s Bay Company opened Fort Victoria beside it in 1864 under George Flett, who saw to its building. The clerk\'s quarters, of 1865, is called the oldest building in Alberta still on its original foundations. The fort closed in 1883 and reopened in 1887. Around it Métis families laid out long narrow river lots from 1865, and Ukrainian settlers farmed to the north from the early 1900s; the whole Victoria District is a national historic site.',
  'The Hudson\'s Bay Company clerk\'s quarters and the 1906 Methodist church on their riverside sites, with a walking path, a cemetery on the hill above (begun about 1896), and the long river lots running back from the river either side, still farmed. The historic buildings are kept locked and are entered only on a guided tour (about 60 to 90 minutes); interior not otherwise checked.',
  'A Hudson\'s Bay post and a mission church on the old Victoria Trail.',
  'A Government of Alberta provincial historic site at 58161 Range Road 171A, County of Smoky Lake: 10 km south of Smoky Lake on Highway 855, then 5 km east on the Victoria Trail. Admission charged; it includes the walking path, picnic tables and the guided tour. In May and June drop-in tours are generally only after 2 p.m.; closed in fall and winter. Parking not checked on the aerial.',
  [W('Fort Victoria, Alberta'),HP('Victoria District National Historic Site of Canada',7744),L('Victoria Settlement: visit','https://victoriasettlement.ca/visit')],
  brick='?',hours='May long weekend to Labour Day: Wednesday to Sunday 10 a.m. to 5 p.m. (open statutory holidays); closed fall and winter'),
N('lac-la-biche-mission','Lac La Biche Mission (Notre Dame des Victoires)','Lac La Biche','ab','worship','standing',54.82334,-112.09085,'site','open','drive','','1853–1963',
  'Oblate missionaries founded the mission in 1853 in what is now the town of Lac La Biche and moved it to this lakeside spot in 1855. It brought farming, boat building and sawmilling to the First Nations people of the area. The Grey Nuns arrived in 1862 to teach, and the Daughters of Jesus from 1905 ran a school for local and boarding children; the sisters taught in the convent until 1961 and a schoolhouse brought to the site ran until 1963. The rectory burned in 2009. It is a national and a provincial historic site.',
  'The 1894 convent, the oldest building on the site, built of timber sawn at the mission\'s own sawmill and used as school, chapel, hospice and home for the nuns and their pupils; it holds a room with part of an original wall painted with algae and calcimine paint, and the finds from the 2009 rectory fire and from the lake. Other heritage buildings and displays are shown on guided tours.',
  'An Oblate mission of 1855 on the shore of Lac la Biche.',
  'About 11 km northwest of the hamlet of Lac La Biche (Lac La Biche County\'s page). Run by the mission\'s own society; admission $15 adult on its hours page. Closed for the season when read, reopening in June 2027 with exact dates still to come; tours outside the season by calling ahead. Parking not checked.',
  [W('Lac La Biche Mission'),L('Lac La Biche Mission: hours and admission','https://laclabichemission.com/hours-admission/'),L('Lac La Biche County: museums and historic sites','https://laclabichecounty.com/p/museums-and-historic-sites')],
  brick='?',hours='From June (2027 dates not yet given): Tuesday to Sunday 10 a.m. to 4:30 p.m.; closed Mondays'),
# ---- Central Alberta ----
N('rocky-mountain-house-forts','Rocky Mountain House fur-trade forts','Rocky Mountain House','ab','fort','ruins',52.362,-114.94583,'area','open','walk','','1799–1875',
  'The North West Company built Rocky Mountain House here in 1799 on ground the Blackfoot used, and the Hudson\'s Bay Company built Acton House about 35 metres away the same year; David Thompson worked from the post on his way west. The posts were rebuilt on four sites in turn (1799 to 1821, Acton House 1799 to 1835, 1835 to 1861 and 1868 to 1875). The 1835 fort was abandoned and burned in 1861; the last, twice its size, was finished in 1868 and closed in 1875, ending the fur trade there. A national historic site since 1926.',
  'The twin stone chimneys of the 1868 Hudson\'s Bay Company fort, the only part of any of the forts still above ground, on the 0.8 km Chimney Trail; the 3.2 km David Thompson Trail runs along the river to the 1799 sites, where excavated postholes, cellars and fireplaces are left open to view. Listening stations, a bison paddock and the Brierley Rapids canoe launch. Chimney material not checked.',
  'Two chimneys are all that stand of the last fur fort on the Saskatchewan.',
  'Parks Canada, Rocky Mountain House National Historic Site, visitor centre at 392077 Range Road 74A, west of the town of Rocky Mountain House off Highway 11A. Admission charged (2025: adults $9, youth free). Pin is the visitor centre as OpenStreetMap maps it; the forts are on the trails from it. Hours from the 2025 activity guide (the Parks Canada web pages would not open); the guide lists the visitor centre as closed from autumn to May 9, 2026.',
  [W('Rocky Mountain House'),HP('Rocky Mountain House National Historic Site of Canada',7827),L('Parks Canada: Rocky Mountain House activity guide and map 2025 (PDF)','https://pcweb2.azureedge.net/-/media/lhn-nhs/ab/rockymountain/WET4/visit/brochures/2025-RMH-Activity-Guide-Draft-EN-Web.pdf')],
  brick='?',hours='Visitor centre and interpretive programs (2025): May 10 to September 7, daily 10 a.m. to 5 p.m.'),
N('stephansson-house','Stephansson House','Markerville','ab','landmark','standing',52.15925,-114.20113,'exact','open','drive','','1889–1927',
  'Home of the Icelandic-Canadian poet Stephan G. Stephansson, sometimes called the "Shakespeare of Iceland", who came to this homestead from North Dakota in 1889 and lived here until his death in 1927. He built a log cabin and enlarged it as his family grew; he farmed sheep and dairy cattle, gave land for the Hola School in 1892 and wrote poems printed in Icelandic newspapers. The house stood empty from the early 1950s and is now a provincial historic site.',
  'A one-and-a-half-storey log and frame cottage in a Victorian Gothic style, painted bright pink with lime green trim, with a verandah with lattice handrail, scrollwork and floral ornament, a dormer and bay window and a cedar-shingled roof with three lightning rods. Inside, restored to its colours: linoleum wall covering, fir floors, the study, pantry, kitchen and spinning room, and his own spinning wheel, bedstead, writing set, piano and stove. Pre-1914 flower beds and plantings round it. Shown by interpreters.',
  'A poet\'s pink homestead in the Icelandic settlement.',
  'A Government of Alberta provincial historic site at 2230 Township Road 371, Red Deer County, north of Markerville and about 30 minutes west of Red Deer. Admission charged (adults $5 on its page). Call ahead to be sure of a tour. Limited parking. The season dates on its page were those of 2025.',
  [W('Stephansson House'),HP('Stephansson House',5892),L('Stephansson House: visit','https://stephanssonhouse.ca/visit')],
  brick='no',hours='Summer (2025: May 17 to September 1): Wednesday to Sunday and statutory holidays, 10 a.m. to 5 p.m.'),
N('brazeau-collieries-nordegg','Brazeau Collieries minesite','Nordegg','ab','industry','standing',52.46713,-116.09032,'site','open','walk','','1911–1955',
  'In 1907 Martin Nordegg staked coal fields here for German investors, and with British and Belgian money and the Canadian Northern Railway formed Brazeau Collieries; the plant and the town were begun in 1911. By 1923 it was the most productive mine in Alberta, nearly all its coal going to the railway. After the processing plant burned in 1950 the company went into debt to rebuild it larger and fireproof, just as the railways were turning to diesel, and the mine closed in January 1955.',
  'On a hillside cleared from the forest: support buildings of 1913 to 1923 (warehouses, small dwellings, a carpenter and blacksmith shop, the lamp house) of frame and brick; a 1932 garage; the 1946 boiler, hoists, pump houses and wash house; and dominating it all the steel, sheet-clad processing plant and briquette plant of 1950 and 1951, with bins, conveyor ramps and crushers, and slack heaps round about. The site is seen only on guided tours.',
  'A coal plant rebuilt fireproof in 1951, four years before the mine shut.',
  'Owned by Clearwater County. Entry to the mine site only on a guided tour (about 2.5 hours, upper and lower mine), booked through the Nordegg Discovery Centre, where tours start; booking ahead by phone is strongly advised. Shuttle parking area as directed by the county\'s page; accessible parking at the mine. The Discovery Centre is open daily 9 a.m. to 4:30 p.m. from the May long weekend to the September long weekend. Tour season dates not given on the page.',
  [W('Nordegg'),HP('Nordegg / Brazeau Collieries Minesite',15529),L('Clearwater County: Nordegg Discovery Centre and mine tours','https://clearwatercounty.ca/p/nordegg-heritage-centre')],
  brick='?',hours='Guided tours Wednesday to Monday (not Tuesday) at 10 a.m. and 2 p.m.; Discovery Centre daily 9 a.m. to 4:30 p.m., May long weekend to September long weekend'),
N('big-valley-roundhouse','Big Valley station and roundhouse ruins','Big Valley','ab','rail','ruins',52.032715,-112.749,'site','public','walk','stations','1912–1922',
  'Big Valley was a divisional point on the Canadian Northern Railway\'s Alberta Midland line, laid through in 1911: it ran traffic between Drumheller and Vegreville and oversaw the line west to Rocky Mountain House. The townsite was surveyed in 1910, the station built in 1912 and the roundhouse complex between 1912 and 1918. The 1922 merger into Canadian National made the line redundant. The Register calls it the only complex of its kind left in Alberta.',
  'The 1912 wooden station, a standard second-class design by Ralph Benjamin Pratt with a trackside bay window, hipped roof and big gable dormers, and the least altered of four such stations left in the province. Beyond it the concrete ruins of the steam yard: the roundhouse walls with their anchor bolts, two locomotive pits, the turntable\'s circular wall and pit, the ash pit, the stores building basement and stairs, the water-tank footings and the sand house under its mound of sand, with interpretive signs.',
  'The concrete shell of a roundhouse the railway merger left idle.',
  'The station belongs to the Canadian Northern Society; the roundhouse ruins are open year round with interpretive signs. The station\'s inside is open when the Alberta Prairie Railway excursion train arrives, and the local museum daily in summer, 10 a.m. to 5 p.m.; to be sure of a volunteer, book by phone with the society. On Railway Avenue; parking not checked. The rail line beside the station is still in use.',
  [HP('Canadian Northern Station Building and Roundhouse Site Complex',5001),L('Canadian Northern Society: Big Valley','https://canadiannorthern.ca/big-valley'),W('Big Valley, Alberta')],
  brick='?',hours='Roundhouse ruins open year round; station museum daily in summer 10 a.m. to 5 p.m. and when the excursion train arrives'),
# ---- The badlands ----
N('atlas-coal-mine','Atlas Coal Mine','East Coulee','ab','industry','standing',51.32930,-112.48279,'site','open','walk','','1936–1979',
  'The last of 139 mines that once worked the Drumheller valley, where the coal era ran from 1911 to 1984, when the Atlas No. 3 and 4 mines closed. Its sub-bituminous coal heated homes and fired prairie railway engines. Thirteen men died working it, four of them on June 24, 1941, when a gas explosion killed three and a fourth died trying to save them. It became a Provincial Historic Resource in 1989 and a national historic site in 2002, and is run by the Atlas Mine Historical Society.',
  'Canada\'s last standing wooden coal tipple, built in 1937 and over seven storeys tall, the largest left in North America; a working pre-1936 battery locomotive; the wash house, supply house, lamp house, mine office and restored blacksmith shop; and the 210-foot underground conveyor tunnel. The train, the mine portal and the tipple are reached only on guided tours.',
  'The last wooden coal tipple in Canada.',
  'At 110 Century Drive, East Coulee, southeast of Drumheller. Run by the Atlas Mine Historical Society. Site admission for a self-paced visit (most spend one to two hours), with paid guided tours on top; book tours ahead, as they fill fast (bookings for 2027 open May 1). Closed for the season when read, reopening in May 2027; group and school bookings year round. Parking lot mapped beside the site (OpenStreetMap).',
  [W('Atlas Coal Mine'),HP('Atlas No. 3 Coal Mine National Historic Site of Canada',11712),L('Atlas Coal Mine','https://atlascoalmine.ab.ca/'),L('Atlas Coal Mine: site admission','https://atlascoalmine.ab.ca/general-admission')],
  th=[51.32907,-112.48046],thn='Atlas Coal Mine lot (OpenStreetMap)',brick='?',
  hours='May to September (2027), daily 10 a.m. to 5 p.m.; fewer days from September to Thanksgiving'),
N('star-mine-suspension-bridge','Star Mine Suspension Bridge','Rosedale','ab','landmark','standing',51.42005,-112.6145,'exact','public','walk','','1931',
  'A 117-metre pedestrian suspension bridge over the Red Deer River, built in 1931 so the coal workers of Star Mine could cross to the mine. The Alberta government rebuilt it in 1958 to commemorate the valley\'s mining history and maintains it. It was closed for almost four years because the support at its northeast end was in very poor condition, and reopened in January 2024.',
  'The swaying footbridge itself, which you may walk across, and the river and badlands either side. Material not checked.',
  'Walk the miners\' swaying bridge over the Red Deer River.',
  'Maintained by the Government of Alberta (Wikipedia); open to walkers since its reopening in January 2024. Pin on the bridge as OpenStreetMap maps it, at Rosedale, southeast of Drumheller. Parking not checked: the town\'s news pages would not open from here.',
  [W('Star Mine Suspension Bridge')],
  brick='?'),
# ---- The south ----
N('brooks-aqueduct','Brooks Aqueduct','Brooks','ab','water','standing',50.52911,-111.85408,'site','public','walk','canal','1912–1979',
  'Built by the Canadian Pacific Railway between 1912 and 1914 to carry water from Lake Newell east across a shallow valley, part of the CPR\'s irrigation scheme to settle its dry land between Brooks and Calgary. On completion it was the largest aqueduct of its type in the world: over 3.1 km of reinforced concrete, 25,000 cubic yards of concrete and two thousand tons of steel, at a time when reinforced concrete was new. Frost cracked it every year and needed constant repair. The Eastern Irrigation District owned it from 1935, and a larger canal replaced it in 1979.',
  'The flume, shaped to the curve water takes in a full flexible channel (a hydrostatic catenary), hung from a trestle of concrete columns about 20 metres high, 1,030 of them along its length; and the inverted siphon that took the water under the CPR main line, now filled with concrete. An interpretive centre stands near it. Self-guided only.',
  'A 3 km concrete flume on 1,030 legs that watered the prairie for 65 years.',
  'A Government of Alberta provincial historic site, also a national historic site: 8 km southeast of Brooks off the Trans-Canada Highway at the Tillebrook Provincial Park turn. Free admission, self-guided; the aqueduct, picnic area and wetland trail are reached on foot from the parking at the site entrance (limited room for buses and motor homes); gravel road and trail. Hours not given on its page.',
  [W('Brooks Aqueduct'),HP('Brooks Aqueduct',2895),HP('Brooks Aqueduct National Historic Site of Canada',17702),L('Brooks Aqueduct: visit','https://brooksaqueduct.ca/visit')],
  th=[50.53203,-111.83800],thn='Lot off Range Road 142 (OpenStreetMap)',brick='no'),
N('medalta-potteries','Medalta Potteries','Medicine Hat','ab','industry','standing',50.03199,-110.64972,'exact','open','drive','','1912–1966',
  'The Medicine Hat Pottery Company opened here in 1912 on the flats between the clay cliffs and the railway, drawn by cheap natural gas and local clay. Local businessmen bought its assets in 1915 as Medalta Stoneware, renamed Medalta Potteries in 1924, and made crocks, jugs and churns, then from 1927 brightly glazed artware. It was the first western Canadian maker to ship goods east of the Great Lakes, and from 1928 to 1950 made on average two thirds of Canada\'s pottery. The national designation says it operated until 1954; the provincial one says it went bankrupt in 1958 and closed for good in 1966.',
  'Four brick beehive down-draft kilns of about 1920 to 1926 with iron banding, chimneys and firing ports; five brick and wood factory buildings of 1912 to 1916 with monitor roofs; and machinery still in place: dry pan grinders, bucket elevators, pug mills, blungers, a ball mill, a filter press, line shafts, a sagger press and a narrow-gauge rail system. Shard piles lie east of the buildings. It is now a museum and working craft centre.',
  'Two thirds of Canada\'s pottery came out of these beehive kilns.',
  'In the Historic Clay District of southeast Medicine Hat. Run as a museum by Medalta; admission charged. Guided tours on Saturdays at 11 a.m. and 1 p.m. in the fall and winter season; its page says hours may change for events. Parking not checked.',
  [W('Medalta Potteries'),HP('Medalta Potteries',3444),HP('Medalta Potteries National Historic Site of Canada',12132),L('Museum @ Medalta','https://medalta.org/visit/museum/')],
  conf='mixed',brick='yes',hours='September to June: Wednesday to Saturday 10 a.m. to 4 p.m. From July 1, summer season: daily 10 a.m. to 5 p.m.'),
N('lethbridge-viaduct','Lethbridge Viaduct (High Level Bridge)','Lethbridge','ab','rail','standing',49.6975,-112.86861,'site','public','walk','','1907–1909',
  'Built by the Canadian Pacific Railway from 1907 to 1909 to replace a long wooden trestle and shorten the line west towards the Crowsnest Pass. The steel was made by the Canadian Bridge Company of Walkerville, Ontario, and took 645 railway cars to bring in; about 100 men raised it from August 1908, and the last girder was placed in June 1909. It is called the largest railway structure in Canada and the largest of its type in the world, and still carries trains. Its construction was named an event of national historic significance in 2005.',
  'The steel trestle striding across the Oldman River valley, seen from below and from the coulee rims. Indian Battle Park in the valley has an information panel on the bridge, and walking trails under the cottonwoods.',
  'The largest railway trestle in Canada, still in use.',
  'An active CP railway bridge: look from the public parks below, never from the tracks. Indian Battle Park is a City of Lethbridge park in the Oldman River valley with trails and Fort Whoop-Up\'s interpretive centre; its page notes parking restrictions from 10 a.m. Pin from Wikipedia, on the bridge; park parking not checked.',
  [W('Lethbridge Viaduct'),L('City of Lethbridge: Indian Battle Park','https://www.lethbridge.ca/parks-leisure-recreation/parks-and-playgrounds/south-parks-and-playgrounds/indian-battle-park/')],
  brick='no'),
N('bar-u-ranch','Bar U Ranch','Longview','ab','landmark','standing',50.419429,-114.243573,'site','open','walk','','1882–1991',
  'Founded under the federal grazing-lease policy by the North-West Cattle Company of Fred Stimson, which ran cattle on open range from 1881 and 1882. The cowboy George Lane took it over in 1902 and raised cattle and Percheron horses; Patrick Burns bought it in 1927 and ran it in his group of ranches until about 1950, when most of the land was sold. At its peak it covered over 160,000 acres. It worked as a cattle ranch until 1991, when Parks Canada bought the headquarters, and opened to visitors in 1995. Harry Longabaugh, later the outlaw "Sundance Kid", broke horses here in 1891.',
  'The ranch headquarters in its coulee along Pekisko Creek: about 30 wooden buildings of 1883 to 1949 in three groups, houses and outbuildings, barns, sheds, bunkhouses, a cookhouse and root cellar, stables, a stud horse barn, a blacksmith shop and a post office, with corrals, fences and the old roads and bridge site. Costumed interpreters work the site.',
  'A working ranch headquarters of the 1880s to 1940s, kept whole.',
  'Parks Canada, Bar U Ranch National Historic Site, near Longview in the foothills. Admission and hours not checked: the Parks Canada pages would not open from here, and Wikipedia says only that it is open daily from mid-May to the end of September. Pin is the Register\'s point for the site; the visitor centre and parking not checked.',
  [W('Bar U Ranch'),HP('Bar U Ranch National Historic Site of Canada',11640)],
  brick='no',hours='?'),
N('lougheed-house','Lougheed House (Beaulieu)','Calgary','ab','landmark','standing',51.0406,-114.07717,'exact','open','drive','','1891',
  'Built in 1891 on open prairie southwest of the young town for James Lougheed, a lawyer who reached Calgary in 1883, became a senator in 1889, led the Conservatives in the Senate from 1906 and was knighted in 1917, and his wife Isabella Hardisty. They called it Beaulieu and entertained royal guests here, among them the Duke and Duchess of Connaught, Princess Patricia and the Prince of Wales. Later it was a training centre for young women, women\'s military barracks and a Red Cross blood clinic, then stood unused until restoration began in 2000. The Register calls it one of the last sandstone residences of its kind in Alberta.',
  'A rough-faced local sandstone mansion by the Ottawa architect James C. Bowes, with a square and an octagonal tower, round-arched windows, tall sandstone chimneys and an Ionic porch. Inside: Spanish mahogany, Italian marble, and stained glass windows painted with Alberta\'s plants and animals, on what survives of the original plan. Three acres of gardens (Beaulieu Gardens) round it.',
  'A senator\'s sandstone castle on the prairie, 1891.',
  'At 707 13 Avenue SW in the Beltline, run by the Lougheed House Conservation Society. Admission charged (adults $8.50 on its page); private guided tours can be booked. Lifts reach every floor. Accessible parking available; other parking not checked. The house observes statutory holidays unless posted.',
  [W('Lougheed House'),HP('Senator Lougheed Residence',5147),L('Lougheed House: hours and admission','https://lougheedhouse.com/new-dropdown')],
  brick='no',hours='House: September to May Friday to Sunday, June to August Thursday to Sunday, 11:30 a.m. to 3 p.m. Gardens daily 7 a.m. to 11 p.m.'),
# ---- The Peace River country ----
N('historic-dunvegan','Historic Dunvegan (Fort Dunvegan)','Dunvegan','ab','landmark','standing',55.9235,-118.604,'site','open','walk','','1805–1918',
  'Alexander Mackenzie noted the spot in 1793, and Archibald Norman McLeod of the North West Company founded Fort Dunvegan here in 1805, naming it after Dunvegan Castle. After the North West and Hudson\'s Bay companies merged in 1821 it became a steady Hudson\'s Bay post, trading furs and growing grain and vegetables on the river flats. In 1878 it became the headquarters of the Athabasca District, which brought new building, but the headquarters moved to Lesser Slave Lake in 1886, and the company closed the post in 1918.',
  'The Factor\'s House of 1877 and 1878, whitewashed squared logs with dovetailed corners (not the usual post-on-sill) on a rock foundation, under a cedar-shingled hipped roof with 6-over-6 sash windows; the archaeological remains of the North West and Hudson\'s Bay posts on the flats; and, beside the site, the St. Charles Mission church and rectory. Four historic buildings in all, entered only on guided tours of about an hour and a half.',
  'A fur factor\'s log house of 1878 on the Peace River.',
  'In Dunvegan Provincial Park on the north bank of the Peace at the Highway 2 bridge, 26 km south of Fairview. A Government of Alberta provincial historic site; admission charged (adults $5 on its page). The grounds and interpretive panels are open at all times; the buildings are locked except on tours, and in May and June tours are not given from 10 a.m. to 2 p.m. on weekdays. An older page on the same site says the centre is closed until further notice; the visit page gives the 2027 season. Campground beside it (Alberta Parks).',
  [W('Dunvegan, Alberta'),HP('Historic Dunvegan',8769),HP('Fort Dunvegan National Historic Site of Canada',18983),L('Historic Dunvegan: visit','https://historicdunvegan.ca/visit')],
  brick='?',hours='May 22 to September 6 (2027): Wednesday to Sunday 10 a.m. to 5 p.m.; grounds open at all times'),
]
